# Derived data. NOT tracked, deliberately — see README "Running the analysis".
#
# Everything here is rebuilt by `Rscript R/01_load_data.R` in seconds from the
# camera-trap parquets, which are the actual source of truth. Two reasons it is not
# committed:
#
#   1. An .rds is an opaque binary. Once in git, nothing in the repository answers
#      "which upstream data is this?", which is the exact question the contract check
#      exists to make answerable. Committing the derived data and its provenance stamp
#      would answer it; committing the data alone answers it wrongly, by looking
#      authoritative while being undated.
#   2. It is a second copy of the canonical table, one commit away from disagreeing
#      with it. The project already refuses to read a second source of truth for the
#      observations themselves; this is the same rule applied to its own outputs.
#
# `contract_stamp.json` goes with them: it describes THESE files, so it is meaningless
# beside a data/ that a fresh clone has not built yet.
#
# WHAT IS STILL TRACKED, and why: `data/overlap_stats.csv` and everything in
# `figures/`. Those are results, not intermediates — readable, small, and the thing
# you would put in a report. The line is "can a person read it", not "is it derived".
data/*.rds
data/contract_stamp.json

# R writes this when a script plots without an explicit device.
Rplots.pdf
